IMPORTANT — what this covers, and what it does not
==================================================

The MIT terms below apply to Cadenza's own source code. They do not describe
every artifact this project distributes, and saying only "MIT" would be
inaccurate.

Distributed builds are a combined work under GPL-2.0-or-later.
---------------------------------------------------------------
The Docker image and the Synology .spk bundle mutagen, which is
GPL-2.0-or-later and is imported into the same Python process by
backend/app/core/tags.py. Under the GPL, importing a module into your own
interpreter forms a combined work, so those builds are conveyed under
GPL-2.0-or-later as a whole. Anyone redistributing them takes on the GPL's
obligations, including making the corresponding source available.

mutagen is kept because it reads and writes tags across every format Cadenza
supports and there is no equivalent with that coverage. It is the one such
trade in the project, it is deliberate, and scripts/check-licences.py fails the
build if a second one appears.

Cadenza's own source remains MIT.
---------------------------------
If you take this source without the bundled dependencies, the MIT terms below
are all that apply to it.

Bundled programs are separate works.
------------------------------------
ffmpeg, ffprobe (LGPL-3.0-or-later) and fpcalc (MIT AND LGPL-2.1-only) are
invoked as separate processes, which does not create a combined work. They are
redistributed under their own terms, with their licence texts in
doc/licences/ inside the package and their corresponding source published with
each release.

The full inventory, read from each package's own metadata, is in
docs/THIRD-PARTY.md.

